WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
 Now, one of the things that was quite interesting is we in the academic field had the right

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.520
 to use this network, but when it first started out, it was supposed to be academic only.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.400
 And then once it was established in the late '80s and early '90s, there were some people

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.960
 that started sort of like bending those rules or bypassing those rules or ignoring those

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.960
 rules.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.720
 And it wasn't really clear who was supposed to police those rules.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.000
 And the people like at the University of Michigan, their heart wasn't in reducing who would use

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.000
 the Internet.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.640
 They wanted to expand, right?

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.640
 And so there were places, often bulletin board systems would somehow get a connection to

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.400
 the Internet, and then they would give Internet access to a bunch of non-academics.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:47.100
 And so it became increasingly in the late '80s understood by everyone around the world.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.760
 But this is pretty much nerds, right?

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.240
 They can sit with a screen, with a command line interface, with a text-based screen,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.360
 and they can get excited about that.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.360
 I'm like that.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.280
 I get excited about things that are non-graphical because I just make it up in my head.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:08.720
 But we needed something to pop this into the collective consciousness of all humanity rather

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.800
 than just academics first and then nerds second.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.040
 But that was about to change.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.280
 So we started our picture where we had supercomputers at University of Illinois.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.600
 We went to the University of Michigan.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.440
 We got the NSF net funded.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.920
 And now we are going to fly across the ocean to CERN.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.680
 CERN, of course, is a high energy physics laboratory.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.960
 They do this 26-mile circle and smash particles and take pictures of the smashing particles

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.200
 and looking for the Higgs boson or found the Higgs boson.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.960
 It's a really cool place I recommend that you visit.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:52.320
 The CERN is a place that physicists from all over the world visit, live at, and collaborate

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.440
 with.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.160
 For experimental physics, it really revolves around those experimental facilities.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.400
 And so regardless of whether you live in Russia or Australia or Germany or America or Japan,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:08.280
 if you're a high energy nuclear physicist, you have got to work with CERN or spend time

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.320
 at CERN.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.280
 And those who spend time at CERN tend to make the best discoveries.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.400
 And so these projects have such long lead times, and they take so many different kinds

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.400
 of talented people.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.880
 There are metallurgy, welders, physicists, engineers, designers, project managers.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.600
 There's a ton of people involved in it.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.720
 And it's a pretty well-funded operation, and everyone's pretty smart.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.120
 So one of the things that they do is they have fun.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:38.000
 They have these clubs, like the softball club, the cricket club, the blues club.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.080
 And it's kind of like these people are somewhat away from home a lot, and so they just have

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.220
 fun with each other.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.280
 This is a picture that I will show you of the CERNets.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.680
 They are famous for being the first band photo on the web.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.680
 Some people wonder if they're the very first photo on the web.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:58.840
 But they are a doo-wop group, and they sing sort of '50s style doo-wop songs.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.200
 They are very fun to watch.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.080
 But their songs are about like particles and internet and modems and stuff like that, stuff

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.000
 that I care a lot about.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:17.320
 They sadly, they've been doing this since the 1990s, '90, '91, '92 kind of time frame.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.120
 And they all grew up, and their kids grew up, and their kids are all in college.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:25.640
 And so they did their farewell tour in 2011, and one of them's moved to Australia.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.480
 So we don't know if the CERNets are ever going to get back together and sing.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.760
 But for now, they're on permanent hiatus.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.920
 But for your viewing pleasure, take a quick look at one or more of the CERNets songs.

